\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Geometry of the two MSIS scanning the conduit surface $\Omega $ (red mesh); the yellow dashed line is the trajectory of the vehicle. \emph  {Horizontal sonar} (blue, frame $\{S_H\}$): its head rotates about the vertical axis ($\omega ^H$) and points in the direction $\psi ^H$; its beam is narrow horizontally ($\Psi ^H$) but wide vertically ($\Theta ^H = 25^\circ $), so an echo at range $r^H$ can come from any elevation $\theta ^H$ inside $\Theta ^H$. \emph  {Vertical sonar} (green, frame $\{S_V\}$): its head rotates about the longitudinal axis of the vehicle ($\omega ^V$) and sweeps a cross-section of the conduit; its beam is narrow in both directions ($\Psi ^V, \Theta ^V \approx 0$), so $\theta ^V \approx 0$ and each echo at range $r^V$ gives a single point. The ranges $r^H$, $r^V$ are written $\rho ^h$, $\rho ^v$ in the text. Adapted from \citet  {breux2020elevation}.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:def_scanplanes}{{4}{7}{Geometry of the two MSIS scanning the conduit surface $\Omega $ (red mesh); the yellow dashed line is the trajectory of the vehicle. \emph {Horizontal sonar} (blue, frame $\{S_H\}$): its head rotates about the vertical axis ($\omega ^H$) and points in the direction $\psi ^H$; its beam is narrow horizontally ($\Psi ^H$) but wide vertically ($\Theta ^H = 25^\circ $), so an echo at range $r^H$ can come from any elevation $\theta ^H$ inside $\Theta ^H$. \emph {Vertical sonar} (green, frame $\{S_V\}$): its head rotates about the longitudinal axis of the vehicle ($\omega ^V$) and sweeps a cross-section of the conduit; its beam is narrow in both directions ($\Psi ^V, \Theta ^V \approx 0$), so $\theta ^V \approx 0$ and each echo at range $r^V$ gives a single point. The ranges $r^H$, $r^V$ are written $\rho ^h$, $\rho ^v$ in the text. Adapted from \citet {breux2020elevation}}{figure.4}{}}
