\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Surface estimated by the Gaussian process for one scan, shown from two different points of view: (a) and (b) are the same surface seen from two viewpoints. The mesh is the predicted mean range $\bar  {\rho }^{*}(s, \psi ^v)$, placed in 3D along the vertical sonar rays, on a $40 \times 80$ grid of $(s, \psi ^v)$ covering the scan. The colour is the predicted standard deviation $\sigma ^{*}$ of the range, in metres: blue where the wall is constrained by nearby vertical sonar measurements (down to about 0.1~m, the measurement noise), red between and away from them (up to about 0.75~m, the prior uncertainty). Red markers: the vertical sonar points of the scan, used to train the process.}}{13}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:msis_gp_surface}{{7}{13}{Surface estimated by the Gaussian process for one scan, shown from two different points of view: (a) and (b) are the same surface seen from two viewpoints. The mesh is the predicted mean range $\bar {\rho }^{*}(s, \psi ^v)$, placed in 3D along the vertical sonar rays, on a $40 \times 80$ grid of $(s, \psi ^v)$ covering the scan. The colour is the predicted standard deviation $\sigma ^{*}$ of the range, in metres: blue where the wall is constrained by nearby vertical sonar measurements (down to about 0.1~m, the measurement noise), red between and away from them (up to about 0.75~m, the prior uncertainty). Red markers: the vertical sonar points of the scan, used to train the process}{figure.7}{}}
